{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My son, keep my sayings, and store up my commandments with you; keep my commandments and live, and my Torah as the apple of your eye (Proverbs 7:1–2).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, always speaks in the book of Proverbs in the name of Wisdom, and he calls all the commandments in the Torah, which are 613, 'sayings' (אמרים) and 'commandments' (מצות). And so David, peace be upon him, called them 'sayings,' as he said (Psalms 12:7): ״אמרות ה׳ אמרות טהורות״. All the commandments are divided into three categories: received commandments, rational commandments, and commandments whose reason is not revealed and explained. The received commandments are those commandments that a person would not find through his reason apart from received tradition, such as the commandments of tefillin and tzitzit and circumcision and sukkah and shofar and lulav and the like. And all of them are divine commandments, and they are called 'testimonies' (עדות) because they are a complete testimony to divinity and a complete testimony to the creation of the world, such as the commandments of Sabbath and shemittah and jubilee. And from this this category is called in the Writings 'testimonies,' as it says (Psalms 119): ״ואדברה בעדותיך״, (ibid.) ״גם עדותיך שעשועי״. The rational commandments are those commandments that a person would find through his reason even if the Torah had not given them, such as robbery and oppression and murder and theft and the like; and these commandments Scripture calls 'ordinances' (משפטים). Commandments whose reason is not revealed and explained are the commandments of kilayim and meat in milk and the scapegoat and the red heifer, and these Scripture calls 'statutes' (חקים). And this is what is mentioned in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 6:20): כי ישאלך בנך מחר לאמר מה העדות והחקים והמשפטים אשר צוה ה׳ אלהינו אתכם, ואמרת לבנך עבדים היינו לפרעה במצרים ויוציאנו ה׳ וגו׳. Scripture explained that divinity began to become known among the nations when Israel went out of Egypt, and since it became known on our account and He performed wonders with us, therefore we are obligated to accept these commandments of His, which are three categories: testimonies and statutes and ordinances. He mentioned 'testimonies' for the received commandments, and mentioned 'ordinances' for the rational commandments, and mentioned 'statutes' for the commandments whose reason is not revealed in the Torah. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, said here: 'My son, keep my sayings' — for the received commandments; 'keep my commandments and live' — for the rational commandments, through which a person lives and through which the settlements are sustained; and he said, 'and store up my commandments with you' — for the statutes, which are stored away and hidden and whose reason is not revealed in the Torah, but they are a statute and decree of the King, like the matter of the red heifer, which is called חקה. And this is what is written." "<b>This is the statute of the Torah which YHWH commanded, saying: Speak to the children of Israel and let them take to you a red heifer without blemish, etc.</b> This passage is juxtaposed to the passage of the gifts of the priesthood, to teach you that even the purification of Israel is through a priest." "<b>The statute of the Torah.</b> The commentators explained it as the language of decree and royal edict, as a thing whose reason is not known to creatures and they wonder at it, like the matter of the red heifer which purifies the impure and defiles those engaged in its preparation. And the truth is that the red heifer is indeed among the statutes whose reason is not revealed and known, but the essential meaning of חקה is from the language of engraving (חקיקה), alluding to a matter engraved and depicted above and alluded to Israel through the red heifer, and therefore one category of the commandments is called חקים. It says here חקת התורה, and it says regarding the scapegoat (Leviticus 16:29): ״והיתה לכם לחקת עולם״, and regarding kilayim (Leviticus 19): ״את חקותי תשמרו״. And they are further called חקים from the language of boundary, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 5:22): ״אשר שמתי חול גבול לים חק עולם ולא יעברנהו״, (Jeremiah 31) ״חקות ירח וכוכבים לאור לילה״ — that is, the boundaries of day and night; and this is to indicate that we must restrain our thought from speculating about them and not cross the boundary, because of the depth of their comprehension." "<b>And you need</b> to know that there is a difference between חק and חקה, for the commandment called חק is one that derives from the attribute of 'remember' (זכור), in the manner of what is written regarding the Passover of Egypt (Exodus 12:24): ״לחק לך ולבניך״ — for חק is decisive (מכריע), and so in the Book of Formation (Sefer Yetzirah): 'the letters of חק are decisive between the two.' And in the blessing of circumcision: ״חק בשארו״. And in the prayer of the Day of Remembrance (Psalms 81): ״כי חק לישראל הוא״. And the commandment called חקה derives from the attribute of 'keep/guard' (שמור), and this is: ״את חקותי תשמרו״, said regarding kilayim and regarding a brother's wife — and he mentioned the language of guarding (שמירה). And so (Exodus 13:10): ״ושמרת את החקה הזאת למועדה״. And so here he mentioned: ״זאת חקת התורה״, ״והיתה זאת לכם לחקת עולם״." "<b>Which YHWH commanded.</b> It should have said 'which I commanded,' but it is like the language of (Exodus 24) 'and to Moses He said, Ascend to YHWH.'" "<b>Speak to the children of Israel.</b> And it does not say 'speak' (in the plural), because Moses is the principal." "<b>And they shall take to you.</b> The sense of 'to you' is that this was done in the wilderness for that hour. And afterward He would command that it be for the children of Israel and for the convert an eternal statute, to be done for all generations; and so (Exodus 27) 'and they shall take to you pure olive oil,' as I wrote there." "<b>A red heifer, whole.</b> And not 'whole in stature,' for if she were a dwarf she would be valid like other consecrated animals; rather, whole in redness — if she had in her two black hairs or white hairs she is disqualified. For this reason they would purchase her at a high price." "<b>And the Rambam, of blessed memory, wrote</b> in the Laws of the Heifer: the commandment of the red heifer is that she be in her fourth year or in her third, and if she was old she is valid. And one does not purchase a calf and raise it; if they found no heifer but a calf, they appraise its value and it remains with its owners until it grows and becomes a heifer, as it is said: 'and they shall take to you a red heifer.' It is permitted to purchase a red heifer from a non-Jew and one need not suspect that it was copulated with, for a person does not allow his animal to be lost. All blemishes that disqualify consecicated animals disqualify the red heifer, as it is said: 'in which there is no blemish' — this comes to teach that a blemish disqualifies her like consecrated animals, even though she is not offered upon the altar, since Scripture called her a sin-offering. The heifer is exceptional beyond consecrated animals in that labor disqualifies her, as it is said: 'upon which no yoke has come,' and concerning the decapitated calf it says (Deuteronomy 21:3) 'which has not been worked with, which has not drawn with a yoke' — just as the yoke mentioned regarding the decapitated calf, all other labors disqualify like the yoke, so too the yoke mentioned regarding the heifer disqualifies all other labors like the yoke; except that the yoke disqualifies her both during labor and not during labor, while other labors disqualify only during labor. How so? If he tied a yoke on her, even though he did not plow with it, she is disqualified; if he brought her in to thresh, she is not disqualified until he actually threshes with her, and so too everything like this — rode on her, leaned on her, hung from her tail, crossed the river with her, doubled the halter on her, placed a garment on her, even a sheet-wrap — she is disqualified. But if he tied her with the halter because she was rebellious and needed guarding, she is valid; if she did not need guarding, she is disqualified, since anything that does not require guarding is a burden. If he made her a shoe so she would not slip, or spread his garment over her because of flies — she is valid. This is the principle: whatever is for her own need is valid, and for the need of something else, she is disqualified. If labor was done with her by itself, or a yoke came upon her by itself — if it was by his will, she is disqualified, as it is said (Deuteronomy 21:3) 'which has not been worked with,' and if by his will, it is as though he worked with her. Therefore if a bird rested upon her, she is valid; if a male mounted her, she is disqualified, and there is no need to say that a pregnant one is disqualified. If he brought her to fatten her with grain and she threshed by herself, she is valid; if he brought her so she would nurse and thresh, she is disqualified — for she did it by his will, and so too everything like this. A heifer in which a disqualification arose is redeemed, and likewise if she died, she is redeemed on account of her hide, but not to feed her flesh to the dogs. If she was slaughtered for unconsecrated meat, she does not atone. If they purchased a heifer and found another nicer than it, it is redeemed even though it has no blemish. Even an ordinary priest is valid for the heifer in its burning, as it is said: 'and you shall give it to Elazar the priest' — it is a received tradition from the Sages that this was its commandment with the deputy, even though Aaron was still alive, and all the other heifers, whether by the high priest or by an ordinary priest, were burned in the four garments of an ordinary priest. They do not burn the heifer except outside the Temple Mount, as it is said: 'and he shall bring it out outside the camp' — outside the Temple Mount, to the three camps, and on the Mount of Anointing they would burn it. 'And he shall slaughter it before him' — a non-priest slaughters and Elazar watches. 'It' — that he not slaughter anything else with it nor engage in any other labor, as it is said: 'and he shall burn the heifer before his eyes' — that his eyes be upon it until it becomes ash. 'And Elazar the priest shall take from its blood on his finger' — that he not receive its blood in a vessel, but he slaughters with his right hand and receives the blood in his left and sprinkles with his right finger from the blood in his left palm seven times, and he aims his sprinklings from his place opposite the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, and he must be able to see it from there, as it is said: 'and he shall sprinkle toward the front of the Tent of Meeting.' He finishes the sprinklings and wipes his hands on the body of the heifer and descends from the pile and kindles the fire, while the priest stands at a distance and guards it until the fire is kindled in its majority and its belly splits open. And afterward he takes cedar wood and hyssop, no less than a handbreadth, and scarlet wool, the weight of five sela'im, and he says to those standing there: 'This cedar wood, this cedar wood, this cedar wood; this scarlet wool, this scarlet wool, this scarlet wool' — three times for each and every item, and they say to him: 'Yes, yes, yes,' three times for each and every one. And all this for what reason? Because there are seven species of cedar, and four species of hyssop, and the dyed red — there are those who dye it with pua and there are those who dye it with the worm called grana, and the worm consists of the reddest grains, resembling carob seeds, and inside each grain there is a creature like a gnat. Therefore he informs everyone and declares to them that these are the species mentioned in the Torah. And the hyssop mentioned in the Torah is the hyssop that householders eat and with which they season pots. And he wraps the hyssop together with the cedar in a thread of scarlet and casts it into its belly, as it is said: 'into the midst of the burning of the heifer' — not before he kindles the majority of the heifer, and not after it has become ash. Once its burning is done, they beat it with staffs — it and all the woods of the pile in which it was burned — and sift everything in sieves, and whatever black there is that can be crushed and become ash, whether from her flesh or from the wood, they crush until it becomes ash. The water into which they place the heifer's ash — they fill it only in a vessel from flowing springs and from drawing rivers, as it is said: 'and he shall put upon it living water into a vessel.' And the placing of the heifer's ash upon the water that was filled is what is called 'consecration,' and these waters, once the ash has been placed in them, are called 'waters of sin-offering,' and they are what Scripture called 'waters of impurity.' They made additional stringencies in the purity of this sin-offering: a person who is pure, even if he immersed for consecrated food and arose and served upon the altar, is not pure for this sin-offering — not for its burning, nor for filling the water, nor for consecrating it, nor for sprinkling, until he immerses for the sake of the sin-offering, and afterward he is pure for the sin-offering. And likewise the vessel, even the sprinkling-bowl in the courtyard, is not pure for the sin-offering until he immerses it for the sake of the sin-offering. Rabbi Yohanan ben Gudgeda ate in the purity of consecrated food all his days, and his turban was like the lying-area of a zav for the purposes of the sin-offering. And how do they purify those impure from a corpse with the waters of impurity? A pure person takes three stalks of hyssop and binds them into one bundle, in each stalk one sprig, and he dips the heads of the sprigs into the waters of impurity that are in the vessel and aims and sprinkles upon the person or upon the vessels on the third day and on the seventh day after sunrise. And once any amount of the waters of impurity touches any part of the skin of the flesh of the impure person, his sprinkling has taken effect — even if the sprinkling fell upon the tip of his finger or upon the tip of his lip; but if it touched his tongue, it is nothing, even though the tongue is like exposed organs for the purposes of impurity, it is not like exposed organs for the purposes of sprinkling and immersion. Thus far the language of the Master of the Book of Purity, in the Laws of the Red Heifer." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash — and it is written explicitly in the commentary of Rashi z\"l: ויקחו אליך פרה אדומה — 'they shall take to you' — from their own. Just as they partnered in the sin of the calf and removed the golden rings, as it is written (Exodus 32:3), ״ויתפרקו כל העם את נזמי הזהב״, so let them partner for atonement and bring from their own. פרה — a parable: to the son of a maidservant who soiled the palace of the king. He said: let the mother come and wipe away her son's filth; so too let the cow come and atone for the deed of the calf. אדומה — because sin is called red, as it is said (Isaiah 1:18), ״אם יהיו חטאיכם כשנים כשלג ילבינו״. תמימה — because Israel were blameless (תמימים) and through it became blemished; let this one come and atone for them and they will return to blamelessness. אשר לא עלה עליה עול — just as they cast off from themselves the yoke of the kingdom of Heaven. אל אלעזר הכהן — just as they gathered against Aaron the priest to make the calf; and because Aaron made the calf, this service was not given to him, for a prosecutor cannot become a defender. ושרף את הפרה — just as the calf was burned. עץ ארז ואזוב ושני תולעת — three species corresponding to the three thousand who fell in the calf. And cedar is the tallest of all trees, and hyssop the lowest of all — a sign that the exalted one who grew proud and sinned should humble himself like hyssop and scarlet worm and be atoned. למשמרת — just as the offense of the calf is kept in safekeeping for all generations for punishment, for there is no punishment that does not include the punishment of the calf, as it is said (Exodus 32:34), ״וביום פקדי ופקדתי עליהם חטאתם״ — so too this was kept in safekeeping. And just as the calf defiled all who engaged in it — for idolatry defiles, as it is written (Psalms 106:28), ״ויצמדו לבעל פעור ויאכלו זבחי מתים״, and it is written (Isaiah 30:22), ״תזרם כמו דוה צא תאמר לו״ — so too the cow defiles all who engage in it. And just as they were purified by its ash, as it is said (Exodus 32:20), ״ויטחן עד אשר דק ויזר על פני המים״ — so too, ולקחו לטמא מעפר שרפת החטאת ונתן עליו מים חיים אל כלי." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> of Pesikta: פרה אדומה תמימה — 'this is Egypt, as it is said (Jeremiah 46:20), ״עגלה יפה פיה מצרים״.' אדומה — 'this is Babylon, as it is said (Daniel 2:38), ״אנת הוא רישא די דהבא״.' תמימה — 'this is Media, as Rabbi Ḥiyya bar Abba said: the kings of Media were blameless (תמימים); the Holy One, blessed be He, had against them nothing but the idolatry they received from their fathers alone.' אשר אין בה מום — 'this is Greece.' Alexander of Macedon, when he saw Simeon the Righteous standing on his feet, said: 'Blessed be the Elohim of Simeon the Righteous.' The members of his court said to him: 'Are you standing before a Jew?' He said to them: 'When I went down to battle, I saw the likeness of his image and I prevailed.' אשר לא עלה עליה עול — 'this is the kingdom of Edom, which did not accept the yoke of the Holy One, blessed be He, but rather blasphemes and reviles, as it is said (Psalms 73:25), ״מי לי בשמים״ וגו׳.' ונתתם אותה אל אלעזר הכהן והוציא אותה וגו׳ — 'that he is destined to thrust their prince from his domain.' ושחט אותה לפניו — (Isaiah 34:6), ״כי זבח לה׳ בבצרה וטבח גדול״ וגו׳. Rabbi said: His vineyard is great in the land of Edom. ושרף את הפרה לעיניו — this is what is written (Daniel 7:11), ״והיבת ליקידת אשא״. את עורה ואת בשרה ואת דמה על פרשה ישרוף — it, and its refuse, and its camp-followers, as it is said (Ezekiel 27:27), ״הוניך ועזבוניך מערבך מלחיך וחובליך מחזיקי בדקך ועורבי מערבך״ — end of citation." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> Tanḥuma: ויקחו אליך פרה אדומה — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: 'To you — a cow. To you I reveal the reason of the cow, but to others it is a statute (חוקה).' For Rav Huna said: it is written (Psalms 75:3), ״כי אקח מועד אני מישרים אשפוט״, and it is written (Zechariah 14:6), ״והיה ביום ההוא לא יהיה אור יקרות וקפאון״ — things that are concealed from you in this world are destined to be revealed to you in the world to come, like this sealed treasure, as it is said (Isaiah 42:16), ״והולכתי עורים בדרך לא ידעו בנתיבות לא ידעו אדריכם״ וגו׳ ״אלה הדברים עשיתים ולא עזבתים״ — it is not written אעשם but rather עשיתים, for I have already done them for Rabbi Akiva and his companions. Another matter: things that were not revealed to Moses were revealed to Rabbi Akiva and his companions — (Job 28:10), ״וכל יקר ראתה עינו״ — this is Rabbi Akiva. Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 7:23), ״כל זה נסיתי בחכמה אמרתי אחכמה והיא רחוקה ממני״ — אמרתי אחכמה, this is what is written (I Kings 5:10), ״ויתן אלהים חכמה לשלמה״, and it is written (ibid.), ״ותרב חכמת שלמה מחכמת כל בני קדם ומכל חכמת מצרים״. And what was the wisdom of the children of the East? That they were knowledgeable in the constellations and expert in the wisdom of the Rooster — that is the bird about which they interpreted (Ecclesiastes 10:20), ״כי עוף השמים יוליך את הקול״. RSBG (Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel) said: In three things I love the children of the East: they do not kiss on the mouth but on the hand; when they cut meat they cut only with a knife; and when they take counsel, they take it only in the field, as it is said (Genesis 31:4), ״וישלח יעקב ויקרא לרחל וללאה השדה אל צאנו״. And what was the wisdom of Egypt? You find that when Solomon sought to build the Holy Temple, he sent to Pharaoh Necho and said to him: 'Send me artisans for hire, for I wish to build the Holy Temple.' What did he do? He gathered all his astrologers, they divined and saw the people who were destined to die that year, and sent them to him. When they came to Solomon, he divined by the holy spirit that they were going to die that year; he gave them their burial shrouds and sent them back to him, and said to him: 'Did you not have shrouds to bury your dead? Here they are, and their shrouds.' ויחכם מכל האדם — more than the first man, Adam. And what was the wisdom of the first man? You find that when the Holy One, blessed be He, sought to create man, He consulted with the ministering angels and said (Genesis 1), ״נעשה אדם בצלמנו כדמותנו״. They said before Him (Psalms 8:5), ״מה אנוש כי תזכרנו״. He said to them: 'The man I wish to create — his wisdom is greater than yours.' What did He do? He gathered all the cattle, beasts, and birds and passed them before them. He said to them: 'What are the names of these?' They did not know. Once He created Adam, He passed them before him. He said to him: 'What are the names of these?' He said: 'It is fitting to call this one lion (ארי), and this one ox (שור), and this one horse (סוס), and this one donkey (חמור), and this one camel (גמל)' — as it is said (ibid. 2), ״ויקרא האדם שמות לכל הבהמות״. And these are intelligible names, for he grasped with the wisdom of letters to assign names according to the natures of the creatures in strength, swiftness, ferocity, and innocence — and I have already mentioned this in the portion of Bereshit. He said to him: 'And what is your name?' He said before Him: 'Adam (אדם), for I was created from the ground (אדמה).' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'And what is My name?' He said to Him: 'YHWH (ה׳).' He said to him: 'Why?' He said before Him: 'For You are Lord (אדון) of all creatures' — as it is written (Isaiah 42:8), ״אני ה׳ הוא שמי״ — that the first man called Me thus; this is My name, which I stipulated between Myself and My creatures. (I Kings 5) מאיתן האזרחי — this is Abraham, as it is said (Psalms 89), ״משכיל לאיתן האזרחי״. והימן — this is Moses, as it is said (Numbers 12:7), ״בכל ביתי נאמן הוא״. וכלכל — this is Joseph, as it is said (Genesis 47:12), ״ויכלכל יוסף״. The Egyptians said: 'Did this slave rule over us except by his wisdom?' What did they do? They brought seventy slips and wrote on them seventy languages and placed them before him, and he read each one in its own tongue — and not only that, but he spoke in the holy tongue, which they did not understand, as it is said (Psalms 81), ״עדות ביהוסף שמו״ וגו׳. ודרדע — this is the generation of the wilderness, for they were all children of knowledge (בני דעה). בני מחול — children whom the Shekhinah forgave (מחלה) for the sin of the calf. (I Kings 5:12), ״וידבר שלשת אלפים משל ויהי שירו חמשה ואלף״ — Rabbi Shmuel bar Naḥmani said: we searched through all the verses and did not find that Solomon prophesied except close to eight hundred verses; rather, this teaches that for each and every verse he spoke, he brought upon it so-and-so many reasons, so-and-so many parables. וידבר על העצים — he said: why is the metzora purified with the highest of the high and the lowest of the low, with cedar wood and hyssop? Because he exalted himself like a cedar, he was afflicted with tzara'at; and once he humbled himself like hyssop, he was healed and purified with hyssop. וידבר על הבהמה ועל העוף — he said: why is a land animal distinguished by two signs and a bird by one sign? Because the land animal was created from the dry land, as it is said (Genesis 1:24), ״תוצא הארץ נפש חיה למינה בהמה ורמש וחיתו ארץ״, and the bird was created from the waters, as it is said, ״ישרצו המים״ וגו׳, or was created from the mud. ועל הרמש — he said: why is it that of the eight creeping things in the Torah, one who hunts them or wounds them on the Sabbath is liable, while for other creeping things one is exempt? Because they have skins. ועל הדגים — that they do not require slaughter, as it is said (Numbers 11:22), ״הצאן ובקר ישחט להם אם את כל דגי הים יאסף להם״ — these by slaughter and these by gathering. Solomon of blessed memory said: in all these I stood firm, but in the red cow I did not stand firm — אמרתי אחכמה והיא רחוקה ממני, and רחוק״ה in gematria is פר״ה אדומ״ה." "<b>And we find</b> that nine red cows were made from the time they were commanded in this commandment until the Temple was destroyed the second time: the first Moses made; the second Ezra made; Simeon the Righteous and Yohanan the High Priest made two each; the seventh Eliehoenai burned; the eighth Ḥanamel the Egyptian burned; the ninth Ishmael ben Phabi burned; the tenth is destined to be burned by King Messiah, speedily in our days. This is what Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 8:1), ״מי כהחכם ומי יודע פשר דבר חכמת אדם תאיר פניו ועז פניו ישונא״ — מי כהחכם, this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Proverbs 3:19), ״ה׳ בחכמה יסד ארץ״; ומי יודע פשר דבר, this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who expounded the Torah to Moses; חכמת אדם תאיר פניו, Rabbi Yudan said: great is the power of the prophets, for they liken the supernal image of might to the form of a man, as it is said (Daniel 8:16), ״ואשמע קול אדם בין אולי״; Rabbi Yehuda bar Rabbi Simon said from here (Ezekiel 1:26), ״ועל דמות הכסא דמות כמראה אדם עליו מלמעלה״; ועז פניו ישונא — that He changes from the attribute of judgment to the attribute of mercy upon Israel. Thus they expounded in the Midrash." "<b>And furthermore</b> they expounded: four things the evil inclination argues against, and concerning them it is written חקה (statute): a brother's wife, mixed species, the scapegoat, and the red cow. A brother's wife — as it is written (Leviticus 18:15–16), ״ערות אשת אחיך לא תגלה״, and concerning it is written (Leviticus 20), ״ושמרתם את כל חקותי״; and if he died without children and did not give her a bill of divorce, (Deuteronomy 25:5), ״יבמה יבא עליה״. Mixed species — as it is said (Deuteronomy 22:11), ״לא תלבש שעטנז״, yet a linen sheet with tekhelet fringes, which is of wool, is permitted. The scapegoat — as it is written (Leviticus 16:26), ״והמשלח את השעיר לעזאזל יכבס בגדיו״ — it itself goes to death and atones for others, and it is written (ibid.), ״והיתה זאת לכם לחקת עולם״. The red cow — as they taught in the Mishnah: all who engage in the cow, from beginning to end, render garments impure, yet it itself purifies the impure. This is what Scripture says (Job 14:4), ״מי יתן טהור מטמא לא אחד״ — such as Abraham from Terah, Hezekiah from Ahaz, Mordecai from Shimei, Israel from the nations of the world, the world to come from this world. Who did this, who commanded this, who decreed this? The Unique One of the world. There they taught in the Mishnah: a bright spot the size of a split bean on a person is impure — and that is half a broad bean; and if it spread over all of him, he is pure. End of citation." "<b>This Midrash</b> explains to us the great innovation found in these four commandments that are called חקות — whose heart no wise man, however small, fails to grasp the exalted insights contained in them, while the evil impulse stumbles after it. And all the more so in the commandment of the red heifer, one must wonder at it, for in its very essence and substance it embraces a thing and its opposite: impurity and purity — for it defiles all who engage in its preparation, yet it purifies the impure through its ashes. And even the father of fathers of impurity — such as a sword and all other species of metal vessels that touched a corpse — behold, they are purified of their impurity by being sprinkled on the third and seventh days. And how is it possible to produce purity from within impurity, and who can bring clean out of unclean? Therefore he cited the verse: ״מי יתן טהור מטמא״ (Job 14:4). And similarly the plague of bright spots (ספרח/בהרת): a person is impure when a patch the size of half a lentil appears in one place on his body, yet if his entire body turns to bright spots from head to foot — he is pure. Behold, this too is a wonder and a thing to marvel at, and it too is clean from unclean." "<b>And likewise</b> in tractate Niddah, chapter 1: ״מי יתן טהור מטמא״ — this refers to fat from blood: a woman's blood curdles and becomes fat. And for this reason the liturgical poet (הקליר) composed, at the conclusion of the parashah of the red heifer: 'The dead is in the house — the house is pure; the dead departs from within it — the house is impure.' He called the woman's womb 'a house,' and this is the explanation of what we learned in tractate Ḥullin, chapter המקשה: 'A woman whose child died within her innards, and the midwife extended her hand and touched it — the midwife is impure with seven-day impurity, but the woman is pure until the child emerges.'" "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> the red heifer alludes to the Oral Torah, which is the attribute of harsh judgment (מדת הדין הקשה), and it is what causes all impurity. Now this service is given into the hand of Elazar, so that it be performed according to his intention toward the attribute of harsh judgment, and they should not think an evil thought on account of its being slaughtered outside. And therefore he prefaced and said: ״זאת חקת התורה״ — this newly revealed commandment, which you see the Torah required to be performed outside the Sanctuary, do not think its intention is outside the Holy One, blessed be He, far be it — for it is engraved from the Torah; it is the Oral Torah and it is the sixth attribute. And although the matter of the heifer is deep, deep — who can find it? — in the sixth attribute you will find it, for it is what causes all impurity, and likewise impurity extends through six degrees, as I shall explain shortly. And this sixth attribute is the attribute of Isaac, the עקידה from the attribute of Ḥesed, in the secret of ״ויעקד את יצחק בנו״ (Genesis 22:9) — were it not so, it would destroy the world through judgment and consume it in its flames. Therefore the Master of Mercy, exalted be He, whose thoughts toward us are extraordinary in counsel, magnified deliverance — how so? Israel take the attribute of harsh judgment to reconcile it and render it weak; this is the language of ״ויקחו אליך פרה אדומה תמימה אשר אין בה מום״ — and upon which never came the yoke of servitude like the other attributes below it. For so the Temple is called (Isaiah 64:10) ״בית קדשנו ותפארתנו״, and it is written (Psalms 78:61) ״ויתן לשבי עזו ותפארתו ביד צר״, and the cherubim were exiled with the rest of the vessels, yet we find no concealment (גניזה) except for the Menorah and the Ark — but that which is above them is called בתי גנזי (treasuries of concealment), and I have already mentioned this at the end of Genesis. And understand this. And the reason it was slaughtered outside the Sanctuary is because its purpose is to remove the spirit of impurity; therefore it purifies the impure with living waters into a vessel, for thus does purification flow down from the attribute of Ḥesed, which is above that attribute that causes all impurity." "<b>And you must understand</b> that some of the discerning masters of truth were in doubt concerning what was done outside the Sanctuary — whether it was for the supremacy of holiness, so that it be most holy (קדש קדשים) entirely, and its rank greater than all the offerings performed in the Sanctuary to the point that it cannot be compared to them; or whether it was to diminish its holiness, so that it is profane (חול), unfit to be performed in the holy place. And they asked concerning this: if it is most holy, how does it defile the pure? And if it is profane, how does it purify the impure? Some of them answered that indeed it is most holy, and the reason it defiles the pure is because the pure person becomes impure upon approaching the supernal pure holiness above; therefore it defiles the pure, for all the pure degrees are impure relative to it in their proximity to it. Therefore Scripture said (Exodus 29:37) ״כל הנוגע במזבח יקדש״ — that is, shall be consumed (יישרף), for absolute holiness is displaced by the holiness above it, and likewise absolute purity is displaced by the purity above it. And they said: fire repels fire, fire consumes fire — and it is the fire of the Shekhinah, as the Master said: 'He placed His finger between them and burned them,' as found in tractate Yoma. And among the ministering angels we find fire consuming fire, and likewise they said in the Midrash: when Moses ascended on high he reached the station of the angel Metatron, who said to Moses: 'Ascend to the station of the angel Sandalphon.' He said to him: 'I am afraid lest the fire of Sandalphon consume me' — end of quote. Now according to some, it would be most holy entirely; but the correct view, according to what I mentioned above, is that part of it is holy and part of it is profane — part of it is profane in that it removes the spirit of impurity, and part of it is holy, as it is written that it is called חטאת. And therefore they assigned it superior distinctions over other offerings; therefore it defiles the pure from the aspect of the holiness in it, and purifies the impure from the aspect of the profane in it, by removing the spirit of impurity from them. And in order to learn the reason for the heifer — that it is to remove the spirit of impurity — therefore the parashah required mentioning immediately the impurity of the corpse and the other impurities, for the impurity of dead living creatures is only on account of the power that causes death, and this power is called רוח המושל and is also called הר הגדול, which in the future will become a plain in the time of King Messiah, who will burn the tenth part."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :register {:flags {12 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {4 "The phrase חק בשארו is from the circumcision blessing; the Hebrew may be slightly corrupt in the source. Sefer Yetzirah phrase 'the letters of חק are decisive between the two' appears to reference the dual letters associated with that root.", 9 "גראנ\"א rendered as 'grana' (a medieval designation for kermes dye insects); פואה rendered as 'pua' (a plant-based red dye). ספר טהרה resolved as 'the Book of Purity' — Rambam's Book of Purity (Sefer Taharah) in Mishneh Torah contains Hilkhot Parah Adumah. עו\"ג resolved as עובד כוכבים (non-Jew). צ\"ל expanded to אין צריך לומר.", 11 "Aramaic words and phrases from the Pesikta are rendered literally into English; the Aramaic biblical quotations from Daniel and the Targum-style readings are preserved in Hebrew script per the rules.", 12 "Aramaic midrashic fragments (e.g., כהדין סמיא דצפי) are rendered literally into English per the rules. Divine name read as ה׳ throughout; the first-man naming episode uses YHWH per the text.", 14 "The citation לא אחד (Job 14:4) is rendered hyper-literally as 'not one,' following the text; the midrashic reading takes it as a rhetorical question answered by 'the Unique One (יחידו) of the world.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:06.667539535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}